          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How to Remove Stains From Granite",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-remove-stains-from-granite/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you get an oil stain out of granite?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Make a poultice: mix acetone or mineral spirits into an absorbent white powder to a thick paste, spread it a quarter inch thick over the stain with an inch of margin, cover with pinholed plastic wrap, and leave 24 to 48 hours until bone dry. Scrape off, rinse, and repeat if the mark has lightened but not gone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a poultice for granite?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An absorbent powder — diatomaceous earth, kaolin clay, whiting or even flour — mixed with a solvent into a paste. The solvent dissolves the stain inside the stone, and as the paste dries it draws the solvent and the dissolved stain back out into the powder, which you then discard."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do you leave a poultice on granite?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "24 to 48 hours, until it is completely dry and crumbling. The extraction happens during the drying phase, so removing it while still damp leaves the stain mobilised inside the stone. Pinholes in the plastic cover let it dry at the right pace."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you remove a stain from granite permanently?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most stains, yes, though deep oil in coarse light stone can take three or four poultice cycles. Rust is the hardest because the iron is chemically bonded in the stone. What cannot be removed by any poultice is an etch, because the surface itself has been altered rather than stained."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a stain and an etch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A stain is coloured material absorbed into the pores, and it is drawn out with a poultice. An etch is a dull spot where acid has chemically attacked the stone surface, usually with no colour change at all. Etches need re-polishing, and sealing does not prevent them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will bleach remove stains from granite?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can lift ink and some organic stains on light stone when used as a poultice, but it will lighten dark granite and it must never be combined with peroxide or ammonia. For most food and drink stains, 12 percent hydrogen peroxide is the safer and more effective choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to reseal after removing a stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The solvent in a poultice strips whatever sealer was in that area, leaving it more absorbent than the stone around it. Once the patch has fully dried — give it a day — reseal it and feather the application a few inches into the surrounding surface."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
